## Statement refuted

Every isometric wave operator has range equal to the full absolutely continuous subspace of the interacting Hamiltonian.

## Statement references

[[def-nrqmx-scattering-channel-data]]

[[def-borel-sigma-algebra]]

[[def-lebesgue-measure-and-the-lebesgue-sigma-algebra]]

[[thm-lebesgue-measure-of-a-box-of-every-kind]]

[[thm-rationals-countable]]

[[lem-q-and-irrationals-dense-r]]

[[thm-l-p-of-a-sigma-finite-countably-generated-measure-space-is-separable]]

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** $\mathcal H_0=L^2((0,1),\mathcal B((0,1)),\beta;\mathbb C)$,
$\mathcal B((0,1))$ is the Borel sigma-algebra and
$\beta(B)=\lambda_1(B)$ for every Borel $B\subseteq(0,1)$,
$\langle f,g\rangle=\int_0^1 f(x)\overline{g(x)}\,d\beta(x)$ is linear in its
first variable,
$H_0f(x)=xf(x)$ on $D(H_0)=\mathcal H_0$,
$\mathcal H=\mathcal H_0\oplus\mathcal H_0$, $H=H_0\oplus H_0$ on
$D(H)=\mathcal H$, and
$Jf=(f,0)$. Use $U_0(t)f=e^{itx}f$ and
$U(t)(f,g)=(e^{itx}f,e^{itx}g)$.

[A1] Assume the Axiom of Choice ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]).

[A2] Assume Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]).

[F1] Completeness means $\operatorname{ran}\Omega=\mathcal H_{\mathrm{ac}}(H)$,
not merely that $\Omega$ is an isometry
([[def-nrqmx-scattering-channel-data]]).

[F2] Real multiplication by the bounded function $x$ is self-adjoint on all
of $\mathcal H_0$
([[thm-nrqm-composites-real-multipliers-and-free-bounded-potential-operators]]).

[F3] The spectral PVM of $H_0=M_x$ is
$E_0(B)=M_{\mathbf1_{B\cap(0,1)}}$; its scalar measures are
$B\mapsto\int_{B\cap(0,1)}|f(x)|^2\,dx$. On the direct sum,
$E(B)=E_0(B)\oplus E_0(B)$ is a PVM whose spectral integral is
$H_0\oplus H_0$, so uniqueness in the spectral theorem identifies it as the
PVM of $H$
([[thm-spectral-theorem-for-unbounded-self-adjoint-operators]],
[[thm-unbounded-borel-functional-calculus]], [[def-projection-valued-measure]],
[[def-unbounded-integral-against-a-pvm]]).

[F4] For self-adjoint $T$, $e^{itT}$ is a strongly continuous unitary group
with generator $iT$
([[lem-self-adjoint-operator-generates-a-strongly-continuous-unitary-group]]).

[F5] The wave-operator definition inserts $P_{\mathrm{ac}}(H_0)$ in its
approximants and defines completeness by equality with the interacting
absolutely continuous subspace
([[def-nrqmx-scattering-channel-data]]).

[F6] The Borel sigma-algebra of $(0,1)$ is generated by the countable basis of
open intervals with rational endpoints intersected with $(0,1)$; the rationals
are countable and dense ([[def-borel-sigma-algebra]], [[thm-rationals-countable]],
[[lem-q-and-irrationals-dense-r]]).

[F7] The Borel Lebesgue measure $\beta$ of $(0,1)$ is finite, with
$\beta((0,1))=1$ ([[def-lebesgue-measure-and-the-lebesgue-sigma-algebra]],
[[thm-lebesgue-measure-of-a-box-of-every-kind]]).

[F8] A sigma-finite measure on a countably generated sigma-algebra has
separable $L^2$ under Countable Choice ([[thm-l-p-of-a-sigma-finite-countably-generated-measure-space-is-separable]]).

## Counterexample

**Given:** [A1], [A2], [F6]–[F8], the Hilbert spaces, operators, identification $J$, and unitary groups specified above.

1.1 By [F6], the Borel sigma-algebra on $(0,1)$ is countably generated, and by [F7] its measure is finite, hence sigma-finite. [F8] gives separability of $\mathcal H_0$; a direct sum of two separable spaces is separable because pairs from the product of countable dense subsets are countable and dense, so $\mathcal H$ is separable. By [F2], $H_0$ is bounded self-adjoint on $\mathcal H_0$; its direct sum $H=H_0\oplus H_0$ is self-adjoint because its adjoint is the direct sum of its component adjoints. Its PVM is the multiplication family in [F3], so bounded Borel functional calculus gives $e^{itH_0}=M_{e^{itx}}=U_0(t)$ and $e^{itH}=U(t)$, consistently with [F4]. [A1, A2, F2, F3, F4, F6, F7, F8, given, algebra]

1.2 By [F3], every scalar spectral measure of $H_0$ is absolutely continuous, so $P_{\mathrm{ac}}(H_0)=I$. Thus the projection in [F5] drops out. Using $Jf=(f,0)$, the two-space wave-operator approximants are exactly
$U(t)J U_0(-t)f=(e^{itx}e^{-itx}f,0)=(f,0)$ for every
$f\in\mathcal H_0$ and every real $t$. Since $HJ=JH_0$ and both
generators are bounded, direct differentiation gives
$\frac{d}{dt}(e^{itH}J e^{-itH_0})
=i e^{itH}(HJ-JH_0)e^{-itH_0}=0$. Thus the approximants are constant and both strong limits exist with $\Omega_\pm f=(f,0)$. Their norm equals $\|f\|$, so they are isometries. [F2, F3, F4, F5, given, algebra]

1.3 By [F3], every vector $f\oplus g\in\mathcal H$ has scalar spectral measure
$B\longmapsto\int_{B\cap(0,1)}(|f(x)|^2+|g(x)|^2)\,dx$, which is absolutely continuous with respect to Lebesgue measure. Therefore $\mathcal H_{\mathrm{ac}}(H)=\mathcal H$. [F3, given, algebra]

2.1 The wave-operator range is $\mathcal H_0\oplus\{0\}$, which is a proper subspace of $\mathcal H$ because the constant function $1\in L^2((0,1))$ has norm one, so
$0\oplus1$ is a nonzero vector outside the range. Hence the wave operators are isometric but not complete, refuting the statement. [F1, step 1.2, step 1.3] ∎
